# Lattice transference via reduced bases

This file was automatically generated by Codex.

The classical transference principle compares complementary successive minima
of a full lattice and its Euclidean dual, with respect to a symmetric convex
body and its polar. `successiveMinimaProduct` proves the lower bound one and
an explicit nonsharp upper bound `d * 16^d`, hence `(100*d)^(10*d)`, for every
positive dimension. The formulation covers arbitrary finite-dimensional real
inner-product spaces and compact symmetric convex bodies with nonempty interior.
Its types, hypotheses, conclusions and local definitions use only Mathlib.

The proof develops integer basis extension, shortest-projection size reduction,
primal and dual basis estimates, attainment of minima, and a maximum-determinant
ellipsoid comparison, and the quantitative coefficient-box application.

Verified source: `blueprint/formalization_blueprint.tex`, lem:integer-basis
(lines 257–263), lem:reduced-basis (lines 267–292), and lem:transference
(lines 296–314). Background library references: pinned
[Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Group.GeometryOfNumbers](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Group/GeometryOfNumbers.html)
(Blichfeldt and Minkowski's first theorem), and
[Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.BilinearForm.DualLattice](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/BilinearForm/DualLattice.html)
(dual lattice and dual basis identities). The successive-minima product estimate
is proved here, rather than imported from those modules.
-/
